    <li class="lt-chem-3606">CH<sub>4</sub> (<em>methane</em>, not carbon tetrahydride)</li>
</ul>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Chem1_(Lower)/04:_The_Basics_of_Chemistry/4.05:_Introduction_to_Chemical_Nomenclature" class="mt-section" id="section_4"><span id="Naming_the_ions"></span><h2 class="lt-chem-3606 editable">Naming the ions</h2>

<p class="lt-chem-3606">An <dfn>ion</dfn> is an electrically charged atom or molecule&mdash; that is, one in which the number of electrons differs from the number of nuclear protons. Many simple compounds can be regarded, at least in a formal way, as being made up of a pair of ions having opposite charge signs. The positive ions, also known as <dfn>cations</dfn>, are mostly those of metallic elements which simply take the name of the element itself.</p>

