<p class="lt-chem-3606">(Later on, when you study acids and bases, you will learn that the first two represent the same chemical species.)</p>

<p class="lt-chem-3606">Some of the metallic ions are <dfn>multivalent</dfn>, meaning that they can exhibit more than one electric charge. For these there are systematic names that use Roman numerals, and the much older and less cumbersome common names that mostly employ the Latin names of the elements, using the endings -<em>ous</em> and -<em>ic</em> to denote the lower and higher charges, respectively (Table \(\PageIndex{4}\)). (In cases where more than two charge values are possible, the systematic names are used.)</p>

