<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Chem1_(Lower)/04:_The_Basics_of_Chemistry/4.05:_Introduction_to_Chemical_Nomenclature" class="mt-section" id="section_7"><span id="Acids"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-3606 editable">Acids</h3>

<p class="lt-chem-3606">Most acids can be regarded as a combination of a hydrogen ion H<sup>+</sup> with an anion; the name of the anion is reflected in the name of the acid. Notice, in the case of the oxyacids, how the anion suffixes <em>-ate</em> and <em>-ite</em> become <em>-ic</em> and <em>-ous</em>, respectively, in the acid name.</p>

