</div></div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Chem1_(Lower)/04:_The_Basics_of_Chemistry/4.05:_Introduction_to_Chemical_Nomenclature" class="mt-section" id="section_8"><span id="Organic_compounds"></span><h2 class="lt-chem-3606 editable">Organic compounds</h2>

<p class="lt-chem-3606">Since organic (carbon) compounds constitute the vast majority of all known chemical substances, organic nomenclature is a huge subject in itself. We present here only the very basic part of it that you need to know in first-year chemistry&mdash; much more awaits those of you who are to experience the pleasures of an organic chemistry course later on. The simplest organic compounds are built of straight chains of carbon atoms which are named by means of prefixes that denote the number of carbons in the chain. Using the convention C<em><sub>n</sub></em>to denote a straight chain of <em>n</em> atoms (don't even <em>ask</em> about branched chains!), the prefixes for chain lengths from 1 through 10 are given here:</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table" align="center" border="1" cellpadding="2" cellspacing="2" style="width: 100%;" width="74%">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th scope="col" style="text-align:center;">C<sub>1</sub></th>
