            <td style="text-align:center;" class="lt-chem-3606">hept-</td>
            <td style="text-align:center;" class="lt-chem-3606">oct-</td>
            <td style="text-align:center;" class="lt-chem-3606">non-</td>
            <td style="text-align:center;" class="lt-chem-3606">dec-</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="lt-chem-3606">As you can see, chains from C<sub>5</sub> onward use Greek number prefixes, so you don't have a lot new to learn here. The simplest of these compounds are hydrocarbons having the general formula C<em><sub>n</sub></em>H<sub>2<em>n</em></sub><sub>+2</sub>. They are known generically as <em>alkanes</em>, and their names all combine the appropriate numerical prefix with the ending <em>-ane</em>:</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table" align="center" border="1" cellpadding="2" cellspacing="2" style="width: 100%;" width="51%">
